outputs.exact_statement:
Let $\psi:D\to\mathbb H$ send $a,c,d$ to $0,1,\infty$, and put $x=\psi(b)\in(0,1)$. Under the boundary and approximation conventions above, $$\label{intro:cardy-formula}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb P(C_n)
 =\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^x[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\,\mathrm dt}
        {\displaystyle\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\,\mathrm dt}.$$

In any fixed buffered annulus or sector of the preceding types, a raw passage count $X$ satisfies $$\label{prelim:passage-moment}
 \mathbb E[\exp(BX)\mid\text{exterior}]\le C_B
 \qquad(0\le B<\infty),$$ uniformly in scale and in exterior states inducing the specified graph and arbitrary boundary partition. The same holds for a fixed finite family of macroscopic passage counts in a fixed geometry.

Suppose $Y_n\ge0$, $\mathbb E Y_n\ge n^{-A}/C$, and $\mathbb E Y_n^2\le Cn^A$, whereas $X_n\ge0$ has uniformly bounded exponential moments at every fixed rate. For fixed $C_0\ge1$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, $$C_\varepsilon^{-1}n^{-\varepsilon}
 \le
 \frac{\mathbb E[Y_n C_0^{\pm X_n}]}{\mathbb E Y_n}
 \le C_\varepsilon n^{\varepsilon}.$$

With the definitions above, $$\begin{aligned}
 2P+2D_*+4(C_0+C_1)&\le o(\log n),\label{exp:rectangle-budget}\\
 C_0+C_1+L_0+L_1&\le o(\log n),\label{exp:corner-budget}\\
 P+\sum_j\log\frac{F_-(a_j)}{F_+(a_j)}
   &\le o(\log n),\label{exp:charge-budget}
\end{aligned}$$ where the finite list of fixed charges in the last inequality satisfies <a href="#exp:charge-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="exp:charge-range">[exp:charge-range]</a> and $\sum_j a_j=1$.

Let a fixed simple rectilinear polygon have $m$ concave and $m+4$ convex true corners, with wire changes in side interiors. It has tile approximants differing by $O(1)$ mesh units such that at least $\lfloor m/2\rfloor$ corners of each relative wire type occur in each of the convex and concave sets, also after global wire reversal. For these approximants, $$\label{exp:polygon-saving}
 3P+D_*+\sum_{v\,\mathrm{convex}}C_{i(v)}
       +\sum_{v\,\mathrm{concave}}L_{i(v)}
 \le\frac12P-\frac32D_*+o(\log n).$$ Each fixed family of negative endpoint charges with magnitudes summing to one has total wrong-to-correct local logarithmic cost at most $-P+o(\log n)$.

For every bounded function $\gamma$ of the two colors and every boundary point $u$ distinct from the four marks, $$\lim_{z\to u}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \left|\mathbb E_{\rm open}
       [\gamma(\operatorname{col}(V(z)))D_{V(z)}]\right|=0.
 \label{four:boundary-residual}$$ Here $z$ in the outer limit is a macroscopic interior location, and any lattice vertices tending to it may be used in the inner limit.

For sufficiently large fixed $L$, under the exterior positive tilt $
u_U\propto U\mu$, $$\mathbb E_{
u_U}(Y/U)\ge c_L>0,\qquad
 \mathbb E_{
u_U}|Y/U|^p\le C_{p,L}
 \label{wall:distant-moments}$$ for every fixed $p>0$, uniformly for large $R_0$ and fine mesh.

For every $x>2R+1$, put $$\label{chordal:martingale}
 M_t(x)=\left(\frac{xg_t'(x)}{g_t(x)-W_t}\right)^h.$$ These processes and their reciprocals are bounded on the stopped range, uniformly in the mesh. For arbitrary stopping rules $0\le\sigma_n\le\tau_n$ in that range and uniformly bounded $\mathcal F_{\sigma_n}$-measurable tests $F_n$, $$\label{chordal:approx-martingale}
 \mathbb E\!\left[F_n\bigl(M_{\tau_n}(x)-M_{\sigma_n}(x)\bigr)\right]
 \longrightarrow0$$ under the original single-strand Dobrushin law.

On every compact capacity interval, the Dobrushin driving functions converge in law, uniformly, to $\sqrt\kappa B_t$, where $B$ is standard Brownian motion and $$\label{chordal:kappa}
 \kappa=\frac{8}{1+h}
        =\frac{4\pi}{\arccos(-\sqrt q/2)}.$$

Away from $\theta\in\{0,2\pi\}$, every limiting branch has the local characteristics $$\label{tree:sde}
 \begin{aligned}
 dW_t&=\sqrt\kappa\,dB_t+
          \frac{6-\kappa}{2}\cot(\theta_t/2)\,dt,\\
 dO_t&=\cot(\theta_t/2)\,dt,\qquad \theta_t=O_t-W_t.
 \end{aligned}$$ Here $t$ is that branch’s radial clock; the martingale identities hold relative to the full causal exploration history.

In its own radial clock, the limiting gap is the unique instantaneous reflected diffusion on $[0,2\pi]$ with interior generator $$\label{tree:generator}
 \mathcal A f(\theta)=\frac\kappa2 f''(\theta)
       +\frac{\kappa-4}{2}\cot(\theta/2)f'(\theta).$$ The forcepoint has no singular push: $$\label{tree:no-push}
 O_t=O_0+\int_0^t\cot(\theta_s/2)\,ds.$$ Equation <a href="#tree:sde" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tree:sde">[tree:sde]</a> consequently holds globally, including collisions, with locally integrable drift.

For every even $k\ge2$ and every compact $K\subset\mathcal D_k$, $$\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \sup_{\boldsymbol u\in K}\sup_{I\in\operatorname{Isom}(\mathbb R^2)}
 \left|\Phi_{k,\delta}(\boldsymbol u)
             -\Phi_{k,\delta}(I\boldsymbol u)\right|=0.
 \label{eq:rr-target}$$

Let $N(a,M)$ be the number of loops of diameter at least $a$ meeting $B(0,M)$. For $\delta$ sufficiently smaller than $a$, and every fixed $p<\infty$, $$\|N(a,M)\|_{L^p}\le C_p(1+M/a)^2.
 \label{eq:rr-count}$$ There are $C,\gamma>0$ such that, with harmless fixed changes of the radii, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P\{\exists L:L\cap B(0,M)\ne\varnothing,
                            L\not\subset B(0,R)\}
      &\le C(M/R)^\gamma, && R\ge10M,
       \label{eq:rr-escape}\\
 \mathbb P\{\exists L:\mathop{\mathrm{diam}}L\ge a,
                       \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(z,L)\le r\}
      &\le C\bigl((r+C\delta)/a\bigr)^\gamma,
          &&0<r<a/100.
       \label{eq:rr-point}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants are independent of the deterministic center $z$.

Couple the cellwise lattice height $h_\delta^{\mathrm{cell}}$ and the height $h_\delta^{\mathrm{geom}}$ defined by the smoothed loop interiors using the same independent loop signs. For every smooth compactly supported neutral density $\rho$, $$\label{q4field:height-comparison-L2}
 \mathbb E\left|
   \langle h_\delta^{\mathrm{geom}}
          -h_\delta^{\mathrm{cell}},\rho\rangle\right|^2
 \longrightarrow0.$$ This conclusion uses only bulk crossing and passage-count estimates.

For every real $\rho\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb C)$ with $\int\rho=0$, the independent fair unit-jump height of critical square-lattice $\operatorname{FK}_4$ satisfies $$\label{q4field:plane-characteristic}
 \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \mathbb E\exp\bigl(i\langle h_\delta,\rho\rangle\bigr)
 =\exp\left\{-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}
  \iint\rho(x)G_{\mathbb C}(x,y)\rho(y)\,dx\,dy\right\}.$$ The same assertion holds jointly for finitely many such tests.

There is a constant $C$ such that, for $0<r<R$, $$\label{q4field:four-arm-bound}
 \limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}\pi_4^\delta(r,R)
 \le C(r/R)^2.$$ The same bound, with a constant depending on the fixed bulk collar, holds inside a domain with arbitrary exterior boundary data.

Let $V$ be a fixed polygon and let its actual wall approximations be as above. Then $$\label{q4field:Q}
 h_\delta\ \Longrightarrow\ u+\sigma\Phi_V
 \qquad\text{as distributions in }V,$$ where $u=0$ for a homogeneous wall and $u$ is the harmonic extension of the boundary values $0,1$ for a mixed wall. All joint integrated moments converge. At every tuple of distinct fixed interior positions, the moment kernels converge to those of the same field.

Let finitely many cells of one finite rectangular grid, separated by positive-width grid gaps, contain normalized wall factors, bounded index factors, and charge factors. Let one cell contain only $$X_\delta(f)=\exp\bigl(i\langle h_\delta,-\Delta f\rangle\bigr),
 \qquad f\in C_c^\infty(\text{cell};\mathbb R).$$ In the expectation of the product, this factor may be replaced with error $o(1)$ by $$\label{q4field:neutral-mean}
 \mu(f)=\exp\left(-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}
                            \int|\nabla f|^2\right).$$ The geometry and all gaps are fixed before $\delta\downarrow0$.
